{%- comment -%}
  newsletter-form.liquid — one field, one action, inline result.

  §9.6: one field. Not first name, not "how did you hear about us" — every
  extra field costs completions, and a name the merchant never uses costs
  them for nothing. The address lands in the merchant's own customer list
  through Shopify's {% form 'customer' %}, so there is no app and no
  third-party script involved.

  The result never clears the page or navigates away: Shopify posts back to
  the same URL and the outcome renders in a region that is always present.
  Always present matters — a message element that only exists after
  submitting would shift everything below it on the way in. Reserved height,
  no CLS.

  The region carries role="status", so a screen reader is told the signup
  worked rather than being left listening to an unchanged page. On error the
  input gets aria-invalid and is pointed at the message.

  Known Shopify behaviour: `form.posted_successfully?` is set for every
  `customer` form on the page, not just the one submitted. Two capture points
  on one page — the footer and a blog list, say — will both report success
  after either is used. It is the platform's flag, not a per-form one, and
  there is no server-side way to narrow it.

  Params:
    id           (string, required) — unique suffix; two forms per page is normal
    class        (string, optional)
    fine_print   (string, optional) — send frequency, unsubscribe note
    tags         (string, optional) — customer tag, default 'newsletter'
    placeholder  (string, optional)
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  comment
    Three distinct ids, not one reused. The <form> and the <input> both carry
    an id attribute; giving them the same value is invalid HTML and points
    every `for=` and `aria-describedby` at whichever the parser saw first.
  endcomment
  assign form_id = 'newsletter-form-' | append: id
  assign field_id = 'newsletter-' | append: id
  assign message_id = 'newsletter-message-' | append: id

  assign customer_tags = tags | default: 'newsletter'
  assign form_class = 'newsletter-form ' | append: class

  comment
    Not `placeholder | default: 'key' | t` — that pipes a merchant's own
    string through `t` as well, and a caller-supplied placeholder would come
    back as "translation missing".
  endcomment
  assign field_placeholder = placeholder
  if field_placeholder == blank
    assign field_placeholder = 'newsletter.placeholder' | t
  endif
-%}

{%- form 'customer', id: form_id, class: form_class -%}
  <input type="hidden" name="contact[tags]" value="{{ customer_tags | escape }}">

  <div class="newsletter__field">
    <label class="visually-hidden" for="{{ field_id }}">{{ 'newsletter.label' | t }}</label>
    <input
      class="field__input newsletter__input"
      id="{{ field_id }}"
      type="email"
      name="contact[email]"
      value="{{ form.email }}"
      placeholder="{{ field_placeholder }}"
      autocomplete="email"
      required
      {% if form.errors %}
        aria-invalid="true"
        aria-describedby="{{ message_id }}"
      {% endif %}
    >
    <button
      class="newsletter__submit icon-button"
      type="submit"
      aria-label="{{ 'newsletter.submit' | t }}"
    >
      {%- render 'icon', icon: 'arrow-right', size: 18 -%}
    </button>
  </div>

  <p class="newsletter-form__message" id="{{ message_id }}" role="status">
    {%- if form.errors -%}
      <span class="field__error">{{ form.errors.messages.email }}</span>
    {%- elsif form.posted_successfully? -%}
      <span class="field__hint">
        {%- render 'icon', icon: 'check', size: 14 -%}
        {{ 'newsletter.success' | t }}
      </span>
    {%- endif -%}
  </p>

  {%- if fine_print != blank -%}
    <p class="field__hint">{{ fine_print | escape }}</p>
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endform -%}
